    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d200">

                <pb facs="0289" n="191" sameAs="frus:frus1932v04/pg191"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">793.94 Commission/313: Telegram</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Minister in China (Johnson) to the
                            Secretary of State</hi></head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Peiping</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1932-08-01T20:00:00+08:00">August 1, 1932—8
                        p.m.</date><lb/>[Received August 2—2:45 a.m.<anchor n="17" xml:id="d200-f1"
                        />]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>883. [Paragraphs 1 through 4 of this telegram summarize material printed in the
                    Minister’s despatch No. 1656, August 2, 1932, with enclosure of epitomized
                    record of statements made by Count
                        Uchida, printed in <ref target="frus:frus1931-41v01/pg_95"><hi
                            rendition="#italic">Foreign Relations</hi>, Japan, 1931–1941, volume I,
                        pages 95</ref>–<ref target="frus:frus1931-41v01/pg_98">98</ref>.]</p>
                <p>5. The above sounds to me like a well-thought-out statement of policy which the
                    present Japanese Government is prepared to defend by force of arms and an
                    interpretation of the application of the League Covenant, the Nine-Power Treaty
                    and the Kellogg Pact to situations of the kind that has been created by Japanese
                    initiative in Manchuria which will bear consideration.</p>
                <p>Tokyo informed by mail.</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Johnson</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
